DressCode
*********

.[perex]
DressCode is the dress code for your PHP code. It keeps your style clean, fixes most issues on the fly, and in the very same run upgrades your code to modern PHP (from PHP 8.0 all the way to 8.6) as well as the latest versions of your libraries. Built on a lossless concrete syntax tree, it touches only what it needs to, and the rest of your file remains byte-for-byte identical. It enforces no dogmatic style of its own; instead, it formats your code according to established standards: [PER Coding Style 3.1 |https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/], PSR-12, Nette Coding Standard, or Symfony. And if you want anything done your own way, simply describe it key by key, exactly as it appears in your code, even differently for each part of your project.

For years, I relied on PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer, taking them for granted. Then came the day I needed to write a single custom rule. Only then did I realize what an ordeal it is when a tool sees code as nothing more than a flat stream of tokens and has to guess its actual structure. DressCode was built so that you can write a new rule in an afternoon and have complete confidence in what it fixes.


A Configuration That Describes Your Code
----------------------------------------

Code style tools often come with hundreds of rules, and DressCode is no exception, offering over two hundred built-in rules alone. But you don't have to painstakingly list them in your configuration. Instead, you simply describe how your code should look: the key identifies a specific construct in the code, and the value defines how it should be written.

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations

braces:
	position:
		class: nextLine
spacing:
	concatenation: '$a.$b'
qualification:
	function:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
```

You can read it right out loud: class opening braces go on the next line, concatenation dots have no surrounding spaces, standard global functions remain bare without a leading backslash, and compiler-optimized functions are imported. Expressing what those last two lines say isn't possible with any single setting in PHP CS Fixer; you have to piece it together through the interplay of two separate rules:

```php
'native_function_invocation' => [
	'include' => ['@compiler_optimized'],
	'scope' => 'namespaced',
	'strict' => true,
],
'global_namespace_import' => ['import_functions' => true],
```

The first rule adds a leading backslash to functions optimized by the compiler and strips it from the rest; the second rule then converts every backslash into an import statement. Over the exact same file, both tools arrive at the same code, except with PHP CS Fixer it requires an intellectual puzzle instead of a straightforward sentence. PHP_CodeSniffer with Slevomat can add a backslash to optimized functions or import all global functions across the board, but importing exclusively the optimized ones is simply out of its reach.

Yet you never have to write hundreds of configuration options. The entire setup hinges on just two simple questions: **What should your code look like?** That is determined by the standard: you simply pick one by name. **And what else do you want from your code besides its appearance?** That's where [sets |configuration#Standards and Sets] come in: `modernizations`, `deprecations`, `cleanup`, `correctness`, `types`, and `compilerOptimizations`. A set represents an intent rather than a static list of rules, and it grows over time: whenever DressCode learns a new modernization, the set delivers it automatically without touching a single line of your configuration. For individual tweaks, a specific key is all you need, and wherever you want to bypass even the standard's default behavior, just write `keep`.

You'll see the full decision path (such as `braces.position.class`) directly next to every finding in the terminal. You instantly know what to configure, what to suppress, or what to inspect in detail using `dresscode explain braces.position.class`. And before committing a rule permanently, you can test it on a single run: `dresscode fix --set hygiene.is_null=forbidden` adds it temporarily on top of your configuration and formats the resulting diff cleanly, ready for a single, focused commit. An overview of all sections and their keys is available on the [Rules |decisions/@home] page.


Not Just Style: Upgrading Code Too
----------------------------------

A tool that truly understands code doesn't have to stop at whitespace and braces. As soon as you bump your minimum PHP version in `composer.json`, the `modernizations` set automatically rewrites your code to take advantage of new language features, and DressCode formats the result according to your chosen standard in the very same pass:

```php .[before]
return trim(strtolower(strip_tags($title)));
```

```php .[after]
return $title |> strip_tags(...) |> strtolower(...) |> trim(...);
```

Upgrading third-party libraries works just as smoothly (see [Upgrading Code |upgrade]): bump dependency versions in `composer.json`, run `dresscode upgrade today`, and your code is rewritten to the new API required by the updated packages, with zero configuration and without disrupting your existing code style. For Nette, the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package provides migrations across all libraries (mostly from version 3.0 up to the latest release): handling renamed classes, constants, methods, and parameters. Dedicated migration packages are also available for Symfony, Laravel, Dibi, and Texy. DressCode consults PHPStan for precise type analysis, ensuring deprecated APIs are rewritten based on what a variable actually is. Formatting and seamless version upgrades are handled by a single tool, with one unified configuration and a clean, predictable diff.


Coverage of PER Coding Style and PSR-12
---------------------------------------

Claiming full standard compliance is easy. Putting that claim to the test against the competition is what actually counts. I took the Laravel framework codebase, formatted it using the `perCs` preset, and handed it over to PHP CS Fixer with its official PER Coding Style rule set. Across 1,696 files, it found only 9 minor spots to change. Conversely, running DressCode on Laravel after PHP CS Fixer revealed 1,201 places where PER Coding Style mandates something different (such as strictly one item per line or trailing commas in multiline lists). Testing PSR-12 against `phpcbf` yielded similar results: DressCode discovered 772 lingering violations, including missing constant visibility, which `phpcbf` reports, but cannot fix on its own. Detailed numbers for Symfony and an in-depth breakdown of these differences can be found on the [How DressCode Works |how-it-works#PER Coding Style and PSR-12 Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer] page.


Speed Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer
--------------------------------------------------

DressCode is engineered to run seamlessly after every file save in your editor. In that workflow, single-file latency is what matters most: DressCode takes roughly one-third to one-half the time of PHP CS Fixer (118 ms vs. 323 ms on a small file). It is noticeably faster across entire projects as well when performing equivalent work. With decisions matching PHP CS Fixer's PER Coding Style set, it requires only 64% to 90% of PHP CS Fixer's time in a single process and 46% to 64% in parallel runs across the codebases of Laravel, WordPress, Symfony, and Nette. Symfony, checked using its own PHP CS Fixer configuration (converted via `migrate config`), completes in 56% of the original time (14.2 s vs. 25.3 s), even though PHP CS Fixer additionally runs over a hundred phpDoc and PHPUnit rules that DressCode does not touch.

The full `perCs` preset does far more than that basic set: it automatically wraps long signatures, complex conditions, call chains, and arrays onto separate lines, while ensuring correct casing for identifiers. In a direct preset-to-preset comparison, it finishes Laravel in 72% of PHP CS Fixer's time and WordPress in 118%, where the formatting rules have the greatest volume of work to do.

Compared to PHP_CodeSniffer, the speed advantage is especially apparent during fixes: `dresscode fix` needs only 36% to 54% of the time `phpcbf` spends on PSR-12. Plain reporting without fixing is faster in `phpcs`, simply because `dresscode check` computes the necessary fixes in memory during inspection. Furthermore, upgrading to a newer PHP takes a fraction of the time Rector requires: on Symfony 3.4, it completes in 1.6 s compared to Rector's 128.5 s. Even with [types from PHPStan |phpstan-types] enabled, DressCode remains significantly faster: on Laravel, it finishes in 4.4 s vs. 10.5 s with a warm PHPStan cache (and 9.0 s vs. 195 s from a cold cache).

Measured in October 2026 on PHP 8.5 against PHP CS Fixer 3.95, PHP_CodeSniffer 4.0, and Rector 2.6 (median of three runs).


